WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:16.127
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Friday, November 13. Three things today: South Korea's foreign minister meets China's Wang Yi in Beijing, the U.S.

2
00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:27.486
Justice Department moves to seize over 1.2 billion dollars in North Korea-linked crypto, and North Korea redesigns its national flag for the first time since 1948.

3
00:00:27.486 --> 00:00:38.496
That diplomatic meeting in Beijing matters because it comes just days before President Lee Jae-myung and Xi Jinping are expected to meet face to face at APEC.

4
00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:46.300
President Lee departs for the ASEAN Summit in Manila on Sunday, November 16, before flying to Shenzhen for APEC.

5
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:56.961
First, the Beijing talks. South Korea's Foreign Minister Cho Hyun met China's Foreign Minister Wang Yi today at the Diaoyutai State Guesthouse in Beijing.

6
00:00:56.961 --> 00:01:09.187
Cho described the Korean Peninsula issue as, in his words, "the vital interest of the Republic of Korea, and the peace and stability of the Korean Peninsula is the common interest of both countries."

7
00:01:09.187 --> 00:01:16.989
Wang, for his part, assessed that the bilateral relationship has "returned to the normal track of improvement and development."

8
00:01:16.989 --> 00:01:27.434
The two sides pointed to a projected one-thousand-fold increase in people-to-people exchanges and more than 40 percent trade growth expected in 2026 as signs of momentum.

9
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:39.967
The talks are explicitly framed as preparation for a Lee-Xi meeting on the sidelines of the APEC summit in Shenzhen. That summit begins November 18 and would mark the first Lee-Xi bilateral in ten months.

10
00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:49.858
Before departing for Beijing, Cho told reporters at Gimpo Airport that the chance of Kim Jong Un appearing at APEC in Shenzhen is, in his words, "not very high."

11
00:01:49.858 --> 00:02:00.302
Watch for what Lee and Xi say about the Korean Peninsula when they meet in Shenzhen. The shape of that conversation will tell you a great deal about where Beijing stands.

12
00:02:00.302 --> 00:02:05.770
The vital interest framing Cho used today signals Seoul wants that message heard clearly.

13
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.205
Second, the crypto seizure. The U.S.

14
00:02:09.205 --> 00:02:21.437
Department of Justice announced today that it has obtained court authorization to seize over 1.2 billion dollars stolen from the Binance exchange and linked to North Korean state-sponsored hackers.

15
00:02:21.437 --> 00:02:30.254
The Justice Department attributed the theft to two operations: a social engineering campaign called FaceStealer, and a separate exchange hack.

16
00:02:30.254 --> 00:02:38.885
Bloomberg separately reported that a broader freeze tied to the same North Korean Binance hacking operation amounts to 2.7 billion dollars.

17
00:02:38.885 --> 00:02:50.806
The FBI, meanwhile, attributed more than 400 million dollars in additional thefts over the past 18 months to North Korean state-sponsored actors using video and image-based social engineering.

18
00:02:50.806 --> 00:03:03.907
A Seoul court this week also sentenced three people to prison for helping North Korean cyber actors create gambling websites, with one defendant receiving four years and the other two receiving three years each.

19
00:03:03.907 --> 00:03:17.940
Taken together, today's actions represent one of the largest single enforcement moves against North Korea's cryptocurrency operations, targeting the funding streams that analysts say help bankroll Pyongyang's weapons programs.

20
00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:26.614
Third, the flag. North Korea has changed its national flag for the first time since the country's founding in 1948.

21
00:03:26.614 --> 00:03:39.157
The official website Naenara began displaying the flag with a new aspect ratio of 1 to 1.65, replacing the longstanding 1 to 2 ratio. The two thin white stripes also appear thicker than before.

22
00:03:39.157 --> 00:03:42.667
The change is embedded in a revised constitution. N.K.

23
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:53.585
News and Yonhap both report this as the latest in a series of moves by Kim Jong Un to build a distinct national identity, separate from the founding era of Kim Il Sung.

24
00:03:53.585 --> 00:04:07.493
State media also confirmed that Kim inspected a second major east-coast naval base under construction on November 12, ordering it completed on schedule and to the quality standard set by the party central. And N.K.

25
00:04:07.493 --> 00:04:19.062
News separately reports that North Korea's November 4 cluster warhead test was faked, with state media releasing doctored photographs from 2022 rather than images of a live test.

26
00:04:19.062 --> 00:04:31.410
The flag change and the constitution revision that contains it mark a deliberate break from Pyongyang's founding iconography, reinforcing the Kim Jong Un era as one defined on its own terms.

27
00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:40.981
Now to Pyongyang. State media's lead today was Kim Jong Un's inspection of an east-coast naval base construction site on November 12.

28
00:04:40.981 --> 00:05:00.746
Kim was quoted in the KCNA report saying: "Modern naval base construction is a major core project directly linked to our state's safety and defense of maritime sovereignty, and must unconditionally be completed within the period decided by the party central and at the quality level demanded by the party central."

29
00:05:00.746 --> 00:05:14.216
The flag redesign, embedded in the revised constitution, was also prominent in state media coverage. N.K. News reports separately that the November 4 cluster warhead test shown by KCNA was faked using 2022 imagery.

30
00:05:14.216 --> 00:05:30.958
And North Korea's Foreign Minister Choe Son Hui issued a statement calling the G7's denuclearization demand "vicious criticism against Pyongyang's constitutional rights," adding that North Korea's nuclear status "can never be a subject of Western countries' debate."

31
00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:40.400
The bottom line: state media is projecting construction momentum and constitutional continuity under Kim while pushing back hard on external pressure.

32
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.460
The rest of the news, quickly.

33
00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:58.897
South Korea confirmed today that Nvidia has reaffirmed its commitment to supply 260,000 graphics processing units to South Korea for artificial intelligence factory buildouts, despite the resignation of CEO Jensen Huang following affair allegations.

34
00:05:58.897 --> 00:06:10.490
The science ministry held a meeting with Nvidia this morning and confirmed that cooperation plans with Samsung Electronics, SK Group, Hyundai Motor Group, and Naver Cloud remain on track.

35
00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:23.261
Plans for NVLink integration with domestic AI chips, AI computing infrastructure, joint research, talent development, and holding the GTC conference in Korea will also continue as company-level commitments.

36
00:06:23.261 --> 00:06:32.871
President Lee Jae-myung held a capital markets policy roundtable at the Korea Exchange today and said: "The most important thing is restoring market trust.

37
00:06:32.871 --> 00:06:40.620
When the capital market is trusted, our companies get properly valued and citizens and investors can invest with confidence."

38
00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:48.370
He pledged governance reform, stronger minority shareholder protections, and steps to close South Korea's valuation discount.

39
00:06:48.370 --> 00:07:04.861
The ruling Democratic Party and the government separately proposed requiring CEO appointments at bank holding companies to go through shareholder general meetings, with approval thresholds of two-thirds for initial appointments and three-quarters for reappointments.

40
00:07:04.861 --> 00:07:17.446
Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, has shed 23 percent in the past month, ranking it as the worst-performing major market globally, with foreign investors withdrawing funds for nine consecutive months.

41
00:07:17.446 --> 00:07:26.994
The Financial Services Commission chairman said the market stabilization fund should be deployed only in "extremely urgent and exceptional circumstances."

42
00:07:26.994 --> 00:07:36.045
On trade, South Korea's export price index rose 0.7 percent in October, driven by semiconductor price gains, while import prices fell 1.8 percent.

43
00:07:36.045 --> 00:07:46.274
President Lee also attended a groundbreaking for a Chungcheong mega-project today alongside Samsung chairman Lee Jae-yong, their first meeting in about three months.

44
00:07:46.274 --> 00:07:52.350
The Samsung chair called the projects "a historic challenge to reshape the future of South Korea."

45
00:07:52.950 --> 00:08:02.674
South Korea's Trade and Industry Ministry has activated emergency management for the Czech nuclear project after domestic contractor negotiations collapsed.

46
00:08:02.674 --> 00:08:11.713
The minister pledged fair and transparent contractor selection and said the government will directly engage Czech counterparts on pending issues.

47
00:08:11.713 --> 00:08:25.988
Korea's presidential climate envoy, Special Envoy Kang Geum-sil, delivered a speech at the Antalya Leaders Summit on November 12, announcing Korea's 2035 Nationally Determined Contribution and its K-Green Transformation strategy.

48
00:08:25.988 --> 00:08:37.645
South Korea held its third consecutive separate memorial for the Sado mine today. Ambassador Lee Hyuk called the site one of government-arranged forced labor under Japanese colonial rule.

49
00:08:37.645 --> 00:08:41.759
Japan held a parallel separate event, maintaining its own framing.

50
00:08:41.759 --> 00:08:55.349
On personnel, the government announced that Oh Hyun-ju, a former third deputy chief of the National Security Office, will be appointed as the 15th president of the Korea International Cooperation Agency on November 16.

51
00:08:55.349 --> 00:09:08.564
A Gallup Korea poll taken November 3 to 5 put President Lee's approval at 41 percent, up from the prior reading. The Democratic Party stood at 36 percent, also up, while the People Power Party fell to 28 percent.

52
00:09:08.564 --> 00:09:21.156
Russia and North Korea have filed applications to operate a 14-round-trip-daily bus service between Vladivostok and the North Korean port of Rason over a new bridge, covering a route of about 203 miles.

53
00:09:21.156 --> 00:09:32.501
Analysts note the buses will likely transport North Korean workers more than Russian tourists. The Asan Institute published an analysis arguing that Democratic gains in the 2026 U.S.

54
00:09:32.501 --> 00:09:42.350
midterm elections create new congressional headwinds for wartime operational control transition and South Korea's aspirations for a nuclear-powered submarine.

55
00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:44.522
On Capitol Hill.

56
00:09:44.522 --> 00:09:58.925
The House passed legislation requiring small businesses to adopt mandatory cyber defense guidelines to counter threats from North Korea and other hostile state actors, including through illicit information technology worker infiltration.

57
00:09:58.925 --> 00:10:00.930
The bill now moves to the Senate.

58
00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:14.632
Beyond the region. South Korea is maintaining its 100 million dollar aid commitment to Ukraine despite an unresolved diplomatic dispute over North Korean prisoners of war captured on the Ukrainian front.

59
00:10:14.632 --> 00:10:21.160
Seoul's presidential office said it expects "progress on issues with Ukraine after useful talks in Paris."

60
00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:33.970
Separately, Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power held a progress review with its Czech client on the nuclear project, with Seoul's industry ministry pledging strict accountability for any party obstructing the deal.

61
00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:49.225
Finally, the markets. The KOSPI closed down about 1 percent, at around 5,291. The dollar bought about 1,336 won. Brent crude was just under 96 dollars a barrel, up slightly on the day.

62
00:10:49.225 --> 00:11:04.120
The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 3.00 percent, last set in October. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Friday, November 13. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
